    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In a one-party state a single party controls government by law or in practice; other parties are banned or allowed only as junior allies, and elections offer no real choice of rulers.</li><li>The form began in Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> between 1918 and 1921, spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> and Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and after 1945 to the communist states and many newly independent countries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and Asia.</li><li>Most one-party states became multi-party systems after 1989–1991, but communist parties still rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, and dominant-party systems remain common.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Article 6 of the Soviet constitution of 1977 named the Communist Party “the leading and guiding force of Soviet society”; its amendment in March 1990 ended the party’s legal monopoly less than two years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved.</p></aside>
    </section>

